      "question": {
        "math_academy_id": "q-127217",
        "knowledge_point_id": "c400d85b-94d5-5fd2-98b4-472ab43c5be4",
        "knowledge_point": "Finding the Unit Tangent Vector at an Arbitrary Point",
        "problem": "Which of the following gives the unit tangent vector to the curve\n $r(t)=⟨2t,\\,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,\\,4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t⟩$ at an arbitrary point where $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )?$",
        "worked_solution": "For a curve $r(t)=⟨x(t),\\,y(t),\\,z(t)⟩$, the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ at the point $P(x(t),y(t),z(t))$ is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $r(t):$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,8\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we calculate the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(2)}^{2}+(8\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)^{2}+{(-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+64{cos}^{2}⁡2t+64{sin}^{2}⁡2t} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{1+16({cos}^{2}⁡2t+{sin}^{2}⁡2t)} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{1+16} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{17}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n Finally, we normalize ${r}^{′}(t)$ and obtain the unit tangent vector to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{17}}⟨2,\\,8\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}⟨1,\\,4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$",
