        "knowledge_point_id": "34342124-8cee-5b70-bf57-64f746af8912",
        "knowledge_point": "Finding Unit Tangent Vectors Using the Rules of Differentiation For Vector Functions",
        "problem": "Find the unit tangent vector to the curve $r(t)=({t}^{2}+2)p(t)$ at the point corresponding to $t=-1$, given that  $p(-1)=i+2\\,j-k\\,$ and $\\,{p}^{′}(-1)=i+\\,j-\\,k.$",
        "worked_solution": "For a curve $r(t)=x(t)i+y(t)j+z(t)k$, the unit tangent vector $T(t)$ at the point $P(x(t),y(t),z(t))$ is given by\n\n$$\nT(t)=\\frac{{r}^{′}(t)}{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find ${r}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=-1:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(({t}^{2}+2)p(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+2)\\cdot p(t)+({t}^{2}+2)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(p(t)) \\\\  & =2t\\,p(t)+({t}^{2}+2)\\,{p}^{′}(t) \\\\ {r}^{′}(-1) & =2(-1)\\,p(-1)+((-1)^{2}+2)\\,{p}^{′}(-1) \\\\  & =-2\\,p(-1)+3\\,{p}^{′}(-1) \\\\  & =-2(i+2\\,j-k)+3(i+\\,j-\\,k) \\\\  & =-2i-4\\,j+2k+3i+3j-3\\,k \\\\  & =i-j-k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the magnitude of ${r}^{′}(1):$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖r(1)‖ & =\\sqrt{(1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize ${r}^{′}(1):$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(-1) & =\\frac{{r}^{′}(-1)}{‖{r}^{′}(-1)‖} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot (i-j-k) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}(i-j-k)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unit tangent vector to the curve at the point that corresponds to $t=-1$ is  $T(-1)=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}(i-j-k).$",
